# Git Reset vs Git Revert & Branching Strategies

### DevOps / DevSecOps Interview Notes (Industry Perspective)

---

# 1. Git Reset

## What is Git Reset?

`git reset` is used to **move the current branch pointer (HEAD) to another commit**. It can also modify the **staging area (Index)** and **working directory** depending on the reset option used.

It is mainly used when you want to:

* Undo local commits
* Remove commits before pushing
* Unstage files
* Clean local history

> **Important:** Reset rewrites Git history. Avoid using it on commits that are already pushed to a shared repository.

---

## Git Reset Types

```
Soft Reset
↓
Mixed Reset (Default)
↓
Hard Reset
```

---

# A. Soft Reset

### Command

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

### What happens?

✔ Removes last commit

✔ Keeps all changes staged

✔ Files remain unchanged

### Example

Before

```
Commit C
Commit B
Commit A
```

Run

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

After

```
Commit B
Commit A
```

Changes from Commit C remain in staging.

---

### Industry Use Case

Developer committed with wrong commit message.

Instead of creating another commit:

```
Reset Soft
Edit commit message
Commit again
```

---

# B. Mixed Reset (Default)

### Command

```bash
git reset HEAD~1
```

or

```bash
git reset --mixed HEAD~1
```

### What happens?

✔ Removes last commit

✔ Removes staging

✔ Keeps files

Changes become unstaged.

---

### Example

Before

```
Commit C
Commit B
Commit A
```

After

```
Commit B
Commit A
```

Files remain in working directory.

---

### Industry Use Case

You committed too early.

Need to modify files before recommitting.

---

# C. Hard Reset

### Command

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

### What happens?

✔ Removes commit

✔ Removes staging

✔ Deletes working directory changes

Everything is lost.

---

### Industry Use Case

Local experiment failed.

Need to discard everything.

---

# Reset Comparison

| Feature            | Soft | Mixed  | Hard      |
| ------------------ | ---- | ------ | --------- |
| Remove Commit      | Yes  | Yes    | Yes       |
| Keep Staging       | Yes  | No     | No        |
| Keep Working Files | Yes  | Yes    | No        |
| Safe               | Yes  | Medium | Dangerous |

---

# Unstage Files

Instead of removing commit:

```bash
git reset file.txt
```

or

```bash
git reset
```

Removes files from staging only.

